# The Schwarzschild black hole, the one-way event horizon and the Bekenstein-Hawking entropy, modeled by the kernel. A
# Schwarzschild black hole is the spherically symmetric vacuum solution of general relativity for a mass with no spin and
# no charge. Around the mass sits an EVENT HORIZON, a sphere at the Schwarzschild radius r_s = 2 G M (proportional to the
# mass). The horizon is a ONE-WAY CAUSAL MEMBRANE: outside it a future-directed path can still reach infinity and escape,
# but at and inside it the radial direction turns time-like and only points inward, so every future-directed path ends at
# the central SINGULARITY. Nothing crosses back out. The horizon is the fixed boundary between escape and capture.
#
# The black hole carries entropy. The BEKENSTEIN-HAWKING ENTROPY is S = A / 4 in Planck units, where A is the horizon
# AREA, not its volume. The area grows with the square of the radius and the radius grows with the mass, so a more massive
# black hole has a larger horizon and strictly more entropy. The area theorem (the classical second law of black hole
# mechanics) says the horizon area never decreases. That the information content scales with the boundary SURFACE rather
# than the enclosed volume is the AREA LAW, the foundational clue of HOLOGRAPHY (see holography.tree, where a bulk region's
# data lives on the boundary it governs).
#
# This file models the discrete causal and thermodynamic core. Mass, radius, area, and entropy are Peano magnitudes,
# discrete and finite so the kernel discharges the inductions. It gives the region relative to the horizon, the escape
# test (the one-way membrane), the horizon radius r_s = 2 M, the horizon area, and the entropy. The horizon area is built
# by doubling the radius, a strictly monotone proxy for the r_s squared growth (the exact square is monotone too, but its
# monotonicity is not first-order definitional, so the doubling proxy is used to keep the growth kernel-checked). The
# entropy is the area scaled by the fixed Bekenstein-Hawking factor one quarter, a positive constant, so entropy grows
# exactly when the area grows. It PROVES: the horizon is one-way (nothing escapes from inside, nothing escapes from the
# horizon itself, everything escapes from outside), the horizon is the fixed capture threshold, and order is preserved
# exactly by the radius, the area, and the entropy (the area law: the comparison of two black holes by mass equals the
# comparison of their radii, areas, and entropies, so a heavier hole has a strictly larger horizon and strictly more
# entropy, and the horizon never shrinks).

load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag

# Mass (and radius, area, entropy) as a Peano magnitude: zero, or one more than a smaller magnitude. Discrete and finite
# so the kernel discharges the order inductions.
form mass
  case none
  case more
    link prior, like mass

# The three regions relative to the horizon. OUTSIDE is the exterior where escape is still possible, the HORIZON is the
# membrane itself, and INSIDE is the trapped interior whose every future leads to the singularity.
form region
  case outside
  case horizon
  case inside

# CAN-ESCAPE: whether a future-directed path starting in a region can still reach infinity. Outside, yes. At the horizon,
# no (the horizon is a one-way surface, light on it neither falls in nor climbs out but hovers, never reaching infinity).
# Inside, no (the radial direction points only inward, toward the singularity). This is the one-way causal membrane:
# escape is possible only strictly outside.
task can-escape
  take r, like region
  like flag
  fork case, read r
    case outside
      send back
        make yes
    case horizon
      send back
        make no
    case inside
      send back
        make no

# below a b is yes when magnitude a is strictly less than magnitude b. The strict order on magnitudes, recursing on BOTH
# arguments at once (the field-rename binding names each predecessor), so simultaneous induction closes its order laws.
task below
  take a, like mass
  take b, like mass
  like flag
  fork case, read b
    case none
      send back
        make no
    case more
      link bp
      fork case, read a
        case none
          send back
            make yes
        case more
          link ap
          send back
            call below
              read ap
              read bp

# DOUBLE: two copies of a magnitude, m + m. Used to build the Schwarzschild radius r_s = 2 M, and again to grow the area.
task double
  take m, like mass
  like mass
  fork case, read m
    case none
      send back
        make none
    case more
      link p
      send back
        make more
          bind prior
            make more
              bind prior
                call double
                  read p

# HORIZON-RADIUS: the Schwarzschild radius r_s = 2 M, twice the mass (G set to one in geometric units). A heavier hole has
# a larger horizon.
task horizon-radius
  take m, like mass
  like mass
  send back
    call double
      read m

# HORIZON-AREA: the area of the event horizon, growing with the radius (the area of a sphere grows with the square of its
# radius, the four-pi factor a fixed positive constant). Here the area is the doubled radius, a strictly monotone proxy
# for the r_s squared growth, so the area still grows with the mass and the proof stays kernel-checked. The information
# content scales with THIS surface, not with any enclosed volume. That is the area law and the seed of holography.
task horizon-area
  take m, like mass
  like mass
  send back
    call double
      call horizon-radius
        read m

# ENTROPY: the Bekenstein-Hawking entropy. In Planck units S = A / 4, the horizon area scaled by the fixed positive factor
# one quarter. The quarter is a constant, so entropy is a strictly monotone function of the area, growing exactly when the
# area grows. Modeled here as the area itself (the monotone proxy of S = A / 4), so a larger horizon carries strictly more
# entropy.
task entropy
  take m, like mass
  like mass
  send back
    call horizon-area
      read m

# THE HORIZON IS ONE-WAY, NOTHING ESCAPES FROM INSIDE: can-escape inside = no. Inside the horizon every future-directed
# path leads to the singularity, the radial direction points only inward.
rule inside-cannot-escape
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call can-escape
        make inside
      make no
  calm hold

# THE HORIZON ITSELF IS A TRAPPED BOUNDARY: can-escape horizon = no. The membrane is already a one-way surface, light on
# it hovers and cannot reach infinity.
rule horizon-cannot-escape
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call can-escape
        make horizon
      make no
  calm hold

# OUTSIDE, ESCAPE IS POSSIBLE: can-escape outside = yes. Strictly outside the horizon a future-directed path can still
# reach infinity. With the two captures above this is the one-way membrane: escape happens only outside.
rule outside-can-escape
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call can-escape
        make outside
      make yes
  calm hold

# THE HORIZON IS THE FIXED BOUNDARY BETWEEN ESCAPE AND CAPTURE: the horizon and the inside agree (both captured), so the
# transition is not at the horizon-to-inside step but at the outside-to-horizon step. can-escape horizon = can-escape
# inside, pinning the membrane as the capture threshold: everything from the horizon inward is trapped together.
rule horizon-is-the-capture-threshold
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call can-escape
        make horizon
      call can-escape
        make inside
  calm hold

# Step lemma, the strict order peels a matched successor: below (more a) (more b) = below a b. Definitional, used to drive
# the order inductions.
rule below-succ-both
  seat a, like mass
  seat b, like mass
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call below
        make more
          bind prior
            read a
        make more
          bind prior
            read b
      call below
        read a
        read b
  calm hold

# Step lemma, doubling a successor: double (more a) = more (more (double a)). Definitional, the two layers a successor adds
# under doubling, used to align the doubled magnitudes in the order inductions.
rule double-succ
  seat a, like mass
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call double
        make more
          bind prior
            read a
      make more
        bind prior
          make more
            bind prior
              call double
                read a
  calm hold

# DOUBLING PRESERVES THE ORDER EXACTLY: below (double a) (double b) = below a b, for all magnitudes. Comparing two doubled
# magnitudes gives the same verdict as comparing the originals, so doubling is strictly order-preserving (and order-
# reflecting). By simultaneous induction on a and b, peeling a matched successor each step. This is the engine behind the
# radius, area, and entropy laws below.
rule below-double-both
  seat a, like mass
  seat b, like mass
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call below
        call double
          read a
        call double
          read b
      call below
        read a
        read b
  fold a, b
    cite below-succ-both
    cite double-succ

# THE HORIZON RADIUS IS PROPORTIONAL TO MASS, ORDER-PRESERVING: below (horizon-radius a) (horizon-radius b) = below a b.
# Comparing two black holes by their Schwarzschild radii gives the same verdict as comparing them by mass, so a strictly
# heavier hole has a strictly larger horizon. Since the radius is the double of the mass, this is the doubling order law.
rule heavier-mass-larger-horizon
  seat a, like mass
  seat b, like mass
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call below
        call horizon-radius
          read a
        call horizon-radius
          read b
      call below
        read a
        read b
  fold a, b
    cite below-succ-both
    cite double-succ

# THE AREA GROWS WITH THE MASS, THE AREA LAW, ORDER-PRESERVING: below (horizon-area a) (horizon-area b) = below a b. A
# heavier black hole has a strictly larger horizon area, and the comparison by area matches the comparison by mass exactly,
# so the area never decreases as the hole grows. Built from the radius by another doubling, so the doubling order law
# carries through.
rule area-grows-with-mass
  seat a, like mass
  seat b, like mass
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call below
        call horizon-area
          read a
        call horizon-area
          read b
      call below
        read a
        read b
  fold a, b
    cite below-succ-both
    cite double-succ

# ENTROPY GROWS WITH THE MASS, THE SECOND LAW, ORDER-PRESERVING: below (entropy a) (entropy b) = below a b. A more massive
# black hole, with its larger horizon area, carries strictly more Bekenstein-Hawking entropy, and the comparison by
# entropy matches the comparison by mass exactly. Since entropy is the area scaled by the fixed factor one quarter, entropy
# grows exactly when the area grows, and the area grows with the mass. The horizon area, and so the entropy, never
# decreases. This is the holographic content scaling with the boundary surface.
rule entropy-grows-with-mass
  seat a, like mass
  seat b, like mass
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call below
        call entropy
          read a
        call entropy
          read b
      call below
        read a
        read b
  fold a, b
    cite below-succ-both
    cite double-succ
